freedreno/ir3: say so when a shader needs more reconvergence points than exist.

`ir3_shader_branchstack_hw()` finishes with

    align(MIN2(v->branchstack, v->compiler->max_branchstack), 2)

and that `MIN2` is a silent truncation. Past the cap the hardware has nowhere
left to park a diverged thread, so the thread is not parked and never
reactivated, and the wave continues with an active mask that no longer says
which threads are live. Arithmetic still produces numbers, the shader still
returns, and no layer reports anything.

**Subgroup operations are where a wrong mask stops being invisible.** `ballot`,
`elect` and the ballot scans do not merely run *under* the active mask, they
are questions *about* it, so a mask that is wrong makes them return wrong
answers rather than run slowly. A shader that ballots to count its live lanes
and scans to find its own index among them -- the ordinary way to build a list
on the GPU -- then writes its results to the wrong places.

**The limit is far easier to reach on a8xx than anywhere else.** It is the only
device in `freedreno_devices.py` with `has_dual_wave_dispatch`, which is what
makes `ir3_calc_reconvergence` run the analysis a second time with
`wave_pair_divergence`, and that pass deliberately treats *every* two-way
branch as divergent (`ir3_reconvergence.c:225`) because a pair of co-issued
waves may diverge where a single wave cannot. A shader with deeply nested
control flow therefore accumulates far more outstanding reconvergence points
here than the same shader does on a7xx. a8xx is also the only generation with
no list under `src/freedreno/ci`, so nothing has ever tested this path.

**It writes to stderr, and not through `mesa_logw()`, and that is the point of
the patch rather than a detail of it.** On Android `mesa_log()` defaults to
`MESA_LOG_CONTROL_ANDROID` (`src/util/log.c`) and goes to logcat -- a ring
buffer that rolls in minutes, and which a host capturing the guest's output
never sees at all. The first version of this patch used `mesa_logw()` and
produced exactly the failure it exists to prevent: a run that said nothing,
indistinguishable from a run with nothing to say. `xmlconfig.c` reaches for
stderr in the same situation and explains why in a comment -- "don't use
XML_WARNING, we want the user to see this!"

**This fixes nothing, and says so.** Spilling the branchstack is the fix, and
it is still an open question in the tree's own words --
`ir3_get_reg_independent_max_waves()` carries "TODO: Could we spill branchstack
or is there other way around? Blob just explodes in such case." What this
removes is the silence. A miscompile of this kind currently presents as a game
rendering the wrong thing, with every layer in the stack reporting success;
after this the driver names it, for every shader and every title.

diff --git a/src/freedreno/ir3/ir3_reconvergence.c b/src/freedreno/ir3/ir3_reconvergence.c
index b0d24342520..171ae6836fb 100644
--- a/src/freedreno/ir3/ir3_reconvergence.c
+++ b/src/freedreno/ir3/ir3_reconvergence.c
@@ -63,6 +63,7 @@
 #include "util/rb_tree.h"
 #include "util/u_worklist.h"
 #include "util/ralloc.h"
+#include <stdio.h>
 
 struct logical_edge {
    struct uinterval_node node;
@@ -420,4 +421,55 @@ ir3_calc_reconvergence(struct ir3_shader_variant *so)
    }
 
    calc_reconvergence(so, false);
+
+   /* Say so when a shader needs more reconvergence points than the hardware
+    * has, because nothing else does.
+    *
+    * ir3_shader_branchstack_hw() ends in
+    *
+    *    align(MIN2(v->branchstack, v->compiler->max_branchstack), 2)
+    *
+    * and that MIN2 is a silent truncation. Past max_branchstack the hardware
+    * has nowhere left to park a diverged thread, so it is not parked and not
+    * reactivated -- the wave carries on with an active mask that no longer
+    * describes which threads are really live. Arithmetic still produces
+    * numbers, the shader still returns, and nothing anywhere reports a
+    * problem. A subgroup operation is where it becomes visible: ballot,
+    * elect and the scans all answer questions *about* the active mask, so a
+    * wrong mask is a wrong answer rather than a slow one.
+    *
+    * The limit is easier to reach than it looks on a8xx. It is the only
+    * device with has_dual_wave_dispatch, which runs the pass above a second
+    * time with wave_pair_divergence, and that pass deliberately treats every
+    * two-way branch as divergent -- a pair of co-issued waves may diverge
+    * where a single wave cannot. A shader with deeply nested control flow
+    * therefore counts far more outstanding reconvergence points here than on
+    * any other generation, and a8xx is also the one generation with no CI
+    * list under src/freedreno/ci.
+    *
+    * **stderr, and not mesa_logw().** On Android mesa_log() defaults to
+    * MESA_LOG_CONTROL_ANDROID (src/util/log.c) and goes to logcat, which is a
+    * ring buffer that rolls in minutes and which a host capturing the guest's
+    * output never sees. A warning about a miscompile that is itself lost is
+    * worth nothing -- this was written with mesa_logw() first and produced
+    * exactly that: a silent run that could not be told apart from a clean
+    * one. xmlconfig.c reaches for stderr for the same reason and says so:
+    * "don't use XML_WARNING, we want the user to see this!"
+    *
+    * This does not fix anything. Spilling the branchstack is the fix and is
+    * still an open TODO in ir3_get_reg_independent_max_waves(). What it
+    * removes is the silence, for every shader and every title, so a
+    * miscompile of this kind is reported by the driver instead of being
+    * mistaken for a game bug.
+    */
+   if (so->compiler && so->branchstack > so->compiler->max_branchstack) {
+      fprintf(stderr,
+              "ir3: %s needs a branchstack of %u but the hardware has %u; "
+              "reconvergence points past the limit are dropped, and any "
+              "subgroup operation in this shader may see the wrong active "
+              "mask.
+",
+              so->name ? so->name : "<shader>", so->branchstack,
+              so->compiler->max_branchstack);
+   }
 }
